PCI: tegra194: route MSIs through the GIC's v2m frame

R39 takes PCIe MSIs in each controller's DesignWare MSI receiver. The
receiver raises the interrupt inside the controller, ahead of the memory
writes the device sent before the MSI. An NVMe interrupt can arrive before
its completion entry is visible, and the command then waits out the 30 s
timeout. The receiver also puts every MSI of a controller on one SPI, so
CPU 0 takes all of them.

JetPack 5 sent PCIe MSIs to the GIC instead. This is that code: NVIDIA's
R35 changes as ported to R39.2.1 on the NVIDIA developer forum, threads
297495 and 380388. With it the MSI is a memory write that travels behind
the device's data, and each MSI's SPI can go to any CPU.

A Tegra234 PCIe device cannot write to the GIC. The memory controller
decodes the write as a DRAM address and drops it. Instead the controller
rewrites a write to the frame's SETSPI address into a 64 MB window at
0x54000000, one 64 KiB page per SPI, and that write reaches the GIC
through the IOMMU. So the device is given the frame's physical address,
and each SPI's page is mapped in the device's IOMMU domain when the SPI
is allocated. The device tree gives the window as the frame's second
reg entry. Without it the frame behaves as on any other platform.

Changes from the forum port: a frame with a window maps no IOMMU
doorbell, since the device never writes one. The window pages are
aligned to the cookie's MSI granule, which also holds for a VFIO
domain's MSI cookie.

Applies to the Linux 6.8 tree in L4T R39.2.1. The device-tree half is in
each product's ark-<target>-overrides.dtsi.
